\name{AssetBasket}
\alias{AssetBasket}
\title{A named group of instruments that is priced, analysed and stored as one}
\description{
\code{AssetBasket} holds a list of \code{BasketMember} objects and reads what it needs about all of them in one list request to UBI: \code{POST /api/instruments/ltp}, \verb{/ohlc}, \verb{/quote} and \verb{/prices} each take the whole basket at once and answer one entry per member. The live members report the basket as it stands now, such as its weights, its day move, its breadth and its biggest movers. The history members line up the members' candles and measure how they move together, such as the correlation matrix and each member's share of the risk.

\code{prices()} gives candles for the basket as a whole, the sum over members of a fixed quantity times each member's candle, so the basket inherits every analysis class an instrument does, from moving averages to \code{sharpe_ratio()} and \code{run_backtest()}. A weighted basket turns its weights into quantities at the first candle of the range, starting from \code{base_value}, which is how a price index moves between rebalances. The open and close are exact. The high and low are the sums of the members' highs and lows, an approximation, because the members do not all reach their highs at the same moment. Volume and open interest have no meaning for a basket and are left empty.

A weight vector, such as \code{weights}, is a named numeric vector whose names are the member labels. A table indexed by time, such as \code{member_closes()}, is a \code{data.frame} whose first column is \code{datetime} followed by one column per member label. A table indexed by member, such as \code{covariance_matrix()}, is a \code{data.frame} whose row names are the member labels.

The generator carries \code{AssetBasket$KIND}, the kind stored with the basket, \code{"basket"}.
}
